# Good News Digest — Cursor Rules
# Python backend project by Rujula (UMich CS, rising junior)
# Stack: FastAPI, PostgreSQL, APScheduler, sentence-transformers,
# HuggingFace transformers, Anthropic Claude API, SendGrid
# Full implementation plan: see implementation_plan.md in project root

---

## Project Context

This is a personal news digest service that fetches articles, deduplicates
them using NLP embedding similarity, filters for positive/constructive stories
via sentiment classification, summarizes with Claude API, and delivers a daily
email. Backend only — no frontend in v1.

Pipeline order:
Fetch → Store → Deduplicate → Score Sentiment → Summarize → Compile → Email

---

## Communication Style

- Treat me like a peer — skip explaining basic Python or SQL concepts
- Do explain non-obvious design decisions, tradeoffs, and ML concepts
- Be direct and concise — no filler
- If there are multiple valid approaches, briefly note the tradeoffs before asking me to pick one

---

## Code Style

- Always explain what you're about to write and why before writing it
- Write production-ready code: full type hints, docstrings, logging, error handling
- Use Python 3.11+ features where appropriate (e.g. `match`, `X | Y` union types)
- Follow PEP 8 strictly
- Prefer explicit over implicit — no clever one-liners that sacrifice readability
- Use `logging` module, never `print` for pipeline output
- All functions must have a docstring explaining purpose, args, and return value
- Use Pydantic models for all FastAPI request/response schemas

---

## Architecture Rules

- Every pipeline stage (fetch, deduplicate, sentiment, summarize, email) lives
  in its own module — never mix concerns across files
- Database logic belongs in a dedicated `db/` layer, not scattered in route handlers
- All API keys and config loaded via `python-dotenv` from `.env` — never hardcoded
- Use `psycopg2` with context managers for all DB connections (no leaked connections)
- All DB inserts use `ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING` on URL to prevent duplicates at
  the storage layer

---

## NLP / ML Rules

- Always explain the model being used and why before writing ML code
- For deduplication: use `all-MiniLM-L6-v2` from sentence-transformers
- For sentiment: use `distilbert-base-uncased-finetuned-sst-2-english` via
  HuggingFace pipeline
- Load models once at module level — never reload on every function call
- Cosine similarity threshold for deduplication: start at 0.85 (tunable via env var)
- Sentiment threshold: start at 0.6 (tunable via env var)
- Always note when a threshold is a starting point that needs tuning

---

## Database Rules

- All queries use parameterized statements — no string formatting in SQL
- Every table interaction should be wrapped in try/except with logged errors
- Use SERIAL primary keys, UNIQUE constraint on article URLs
- Never do N+1 queries — fetch in batches
- Always explain the SQL before writing it, especially JOINs or array operations

---

## API Rules

- FastAPI only — no Flask
- All endpoints must have response_model defined
- Use HTTP status codes correctly (201 for creates, 404 for not found, etc.)
- Every endpoint gets a docstring that appears in /docs
- Async only where there's a real reason (external I/O) — don't async everything
  by default

---

## Error Handling Rules

- Every pipeline stage must have its own try/except — one stage failing should
  not crash the whole pipeline
- Log errors with enough context to debug: include article URL, stage name,
  exception type
- Raise custom exceptions for domain errors (e.g. `SentimentScoringError`,
  `DuplicateDetectionError`) rather than bare `Exception`
- Never silently swallow errors

---

## Claude API Rules

- Model: always use `claude-sonnet-4-6`
- max_tokens: 150 for summaries — no more needed
- Only call Claude after sentiment filtering — never summarize articles that
  will be discarded
- Cache summaries in PostgreSQL `summary` column — never re-summarize the
  same article
- Wrap all Claude calls in try/except with retry logic (max 2 retries)

---

## Testing Rules

- Always suggest a unit test after writing a function
- Tests go in `tests/` mirroring the `app/` structure
- Use `pytest`
- Mock all external API calls (NewsAPI, Claude, SendGrid) in tests — never
  hit real APIs in test suite
- For NLP functions, suggest at least one test with a known duplicate pair
  and one with clearly distinct articles

---

## Anti-Vibecoding Reminders

- If I paste code I don't understand and ask you to fix it, ask me to explain
  what I think it does first
- Flag any concept that would likely come up in a SWE internship interview
  with a comment: `# INTERVIEW: understand this`
- When writing ML/NLP code, always include a comment explaining the math or
  intuition in plain English
- If I'm about to do something that works but I probably won't understand,
  suggest a simpler version I'll be able to explain first

---

## Environment Variables Expected

ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
NEWSAPI_KEY=
SENDGRID_API_KEY=
SENDGRID_FROM_EMAIL=
DATABASE_URL=
DIGEST_RECIPIENT_EMAIL=
SIMILARITY_THRESHOLD=0.85
SENTIMENT_THRESHOLD=0.6
DIGEST_HOUR=7